LOCATION:Neal-Marshall Black Culture Center
GEO:39.16829;-86.516866
SUMMARY:AI and Humanities Research: Risk\, Opportunity\, and Change — A
  Talk by Robert Townsend
DESCRIPTION:Drawing on recent experiments with AI for the Humanities Indi
 cators and a study of historians' adoption of earlier technologies\, Town
 send will discuss AI's strengths and limitations in humanities research\,
  how it might redraw the boundary between assistance and expertise in the
  field\, and why these experiments have nevertheless made him more optimi
 stic about the future of the humanities.  This talk is co-sponsored by th
 e Alliance of Distinguished and Titled Professors (https://alliance.iu.ed
 u/).  Later that afternoon\, Townsend will also give the inaugural lectur
 e in a new series on the current landscape both for the humanities and fo
 r humanities funding:What's Really Happening to the Humanities?(And What 
 You Can Do About It)  4:00 pm\, Bridgwaters Lounge\, Neal-Marshall Black 
 Culture Center  275 N. Eagleson Avenue\, Bloomington\, Indiana  The decli
 ne in humanities majors is a familiar story\, but what explains it? And s
 hould that be the principal measure of success for the field? Drawing on 
 more than a decade of research by the Humanities Indicators\, Townsend wi
 ll offer a wide-ranging look at the state of the humanities\, examine the
  pressures shaping our future\, and suggest ways faculty and institutions
  might respond.Robert Townsend oversees Humanities\, Arts\, and Culture p
 rograms and day-to-day work on the Humanities Indicators (https://www.ama
 cad.org/humanities-indicators) at the American Academy of Arts and Scienc
 es (https://www.amacad.org/). Prior to the Academy\, he spent 24 years at
  the American Historical Association\, in positions ranging from editoria
 l assistant to deputy director. He is the author of History's Babel: Scho
 larship\, Professionalization\, and the Historical Enterprise in the Unit
 ed States\, 1880-1940 (University of Chicago Press\, 2013) and author or 
 co-author of over 200 articles on various aspects of history\, higher edu
 cation\, and public humanities.  These talks are presented by the College
  Arts + Humanities Institute with generous assistance from the College of
  Arts + Sciences and the Ruth N. Halls Humanities Fund. For more informat
 ion\, please visit cahi.indiana.edu (https://cahi.indiana.edu). If need a
 ssistance or if you have an accommodation request\, please email cahi@iu.
 edu (mailto:cahi@iu.edu) or call 812-856-1169.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Drawing on recent experiments with AI f
 or the Humanities Indicators and a study of historians' adoption of earli
 er technologies\, Townsend will discuss AI's strengths and limitations in
  humanities research\, how it might redraw the boundary between assistanc
 e and expertise in the field\, and why these experiments have nevertheles
 s made him more optimistic about the future of the humanities.</p><p>  Th
 is talk is co-sponsored by the <a href="https://alliance.iu.edu/" target=
 "_blank" rel="noopener">Alliance of Distinguished and Titled Professors</
 a>.</p><p>  Later that afternoon\, Townsend will also give the inaugural 
 lecture in a new series on the current landscape both for the humanities 
 and for humanities funding:</p><p><strong>What's Really Happening to the 
 Humanities?</strong><br /><strong>(And What You Can Do About It)</strong>
 </p><p>  4:00 pm\, Bridgwaters Lounge\, Neal-Marshall Black Culture Cente
 r<br />  275 N. Eagleson Avenue\, Bloomington\, Indiana</p><p>  The decli
 ne in humanities majors is a familiar story\, but what explains it? And s
 hould that be the principal measure of success for the field? Drawing on 
 more than a decade of research by the Humanities Indicators\, Townsend wi
 ll offer a wide-ranging look at the state of the humanities\, examine the
  pressures shaping our future\, and suggest ways faculty and institutions
  might respond.</p><p><strong>Robert Townsend</strong> oversees Humanitie
 s\, Arts\, and Culture programs and day-to-day work on the <a href="https
 ://www.amacad.org/humanities-indicators">Humanities Indicators</a> at the
  <a href="https://www.amacad.org/">American Academy of Arts and Sciences<
 /a>. Prior to the Academy\, he spent 24 years at the American Historical 
 Association\, in positions ranging from editorial assistant to deputy dir
 ector. He is the author of <a href="https://press.uchicago.edu/ucp/books/
 book/chicago/H/bo14312740.html"><em>History's Babel: Scholarship\, Profes
 sionalization\, and the Historical Enterprise in the United States\, 1880
 -1940</em></a> (University of Chicago Press\, 2013) and author or co-auth
 or of over 200 articles on various aspects of history\, higher education\
 , and public humanities.</p><p>  These talks are presented by the College
  Arts + Humanities Institute with generous assistance from the College of
  Arts + Sciences and the Ruth N. Halls Humanities Fund. For more informat
 ion\, please visit <a href="https://cahi.indiana.edu">cahi.indiana.edu</a
 >. If need assistance or if you have an accommodation request\, please em
 ail <a href="mailto:cahi@iu.edu">cahi@iu.edu</a> or call 812-856-1169.</p
