SUMMARY:The Current Landscape of the Humanities with Robert Townsend
DESCRIPTION:Full description:  This year\, CAHI has organized a series of
  talks on the current landscape both for the humanities and for humanitie
 s funding. On December 4\, Robert Townsend\, Program Director for Humanit
 ies\, Arts\, and Culture at the American Academy of Arts and Sciences\, w
 ill give the inaugural lecture in the series.What's Really Happening to t
 he Humanities?(And What You Can Do About It)  4:00 pm\, Bridgwaters Loung
 e\, Neal-Marshall Black Culture Center  275 N. Eagleson Avenue\, Blooming
 ton\, Indiana  The decline in humanities majors is a familiar story\, but
  what explains it? And should that be the principal measure of success fo
 r the field? Drawing on more than a decade of research by the Humanities 
 Indicators\, Townsend will offer a wide-ranging look at the state of the 
 humanities\, examine the pressures shaping our future\, and suggest ways 
 faculty and institutions might respond.  Earlier in the day\, Townsend wi
 ll give a talk on artificial intelligence and the humanities\, co-sponsor
 ed by the Alliance of Distinguished and Titled Professors (https://allian
 ce.iu.edu/):AI and Humanities Research: Risk\, Opportunity\, and Change  
 1:00 pm\, Bridgwaters Lounge\, Neal-Marshall Black Culture Center  275 N.
  Eagleson Avenue\, Bloomington\, Indiana  Drawing on recent experiments w
 ith AI for the Humanities Indicators and a study of historians' adoption 
 of earlier technologies\, Townsend will discuss AI's strengths and limita
 tions in humanities research\, how it might redraw the boundary between a
 ssistance and expertise in the field\, and why these experiments have nev
 ertheless made him more optimistic about the future of the humanities.Rob
 ert Townsend oversees Humanities\, Arts\, and Culture programs and day-to
 -day work on the Humanities Indicators (https://www.amacad.org/humanities
 -indicators) at the American Academy of Arts and Sciences (https://www.am
 acad.org/). Prior to the Academy\, he spent 24 years at the American Hist
 orical Association\, in positions ranging from editorial assistant to dep
 uty director. He is the author of History's Babel: Scholarship\, Professi
 onalization\, and the Historical Enterprise in the United States\, 1880-1
 940 (https://press.uchicago.edu/ucp/books/book/chicago/H/bo14312740.html)
  (University of Chicago Press\, 2013) and author or co-author of over 200
  articles on various aspects of history\, higher education\, and public h
 umanities.Save these dates for more in the series:February 5: Claire Katz
   President\, Board of Directors\, National Humanities AllianceMarch 24: 
 Phillip Brian Harper  Vice President\, Programs and Higher Learning\, Mel
 lon FoundationApril 8: Joy Connolly  President\, American Council of Lear
 ned Societies  These talks are presented by the College Arts + Humanities
  Institute with generous assistance from the College of Arts + Sciences a
 nd the Ruth N. Halls Humanities Fund. For more information\, please visit
  cahi.indiana.edu (https://cahi.indiana.edu). If need assistance or if yo
 u have an accommodation request\, please email cahi@iu.edu (mailto:mailto
 :cahi@iu.edu) or call 812-856-1169.  For those who cannot attend in perso
 n\, a Zoom option will be available. Please email cahi@iu.edu (mailto:cah
 i@iu.edu) for the Zoom link.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Full description:</strong></p><p>
   This year\, CAHI has organized a series of talks on the current landsca
 pe both for the humanities and for humanities funding. On December 4\, <a
  href="https://www.amacad.org/person/robert-b-townsend" target="_blank" r
 el="noopener"><strong>Robert Townsend</strong></a>\, Program Director for
  Humanities\, Arts\, and Culture at the American Academy of Arts and Scie
 nces\, will give the inaugural lecture in the series.</p><p><strong>What'
 s Really Happening to the Humanities?</strong><br /><strong>(And What You
  Can Do About It)</strong></p><p>  4:00 pm\, Bridgwaters Lounge\, Neal-Ma
 rshall Black Culture Center<br />  275 N. Eagleson Avenue\, Bloomington\,
  Indiana</p><p>  The decline in humanities majors is a familiar story\, b
 ut what explains it? And should that be the principal measure of success 
 for the field? Drawing on more than a decade of research by the Humanitie
 s Indicators\, Townsend will offer a wide-ranging look at the state of th
 e humanities\, examine the pressures shaping our future\, and suggest way
 s faculty and institutions might respond.</p><p>  Earlier in the day\, To
 wnsend will give a talk on artificial intelligence and the humanities\, c
 o-sponsored by the <a href="https://alliance.iu.edu/" target="_blank" rel
 ="noopener">Alliance of Distinguished and Titled Professors</a>:</p><p><s
 trong>AI and Humanities Research: Risk\, Opportunity\, and Change</strong
 ></p><p>  1:00 pm\, Bridgwaters Lounge\, Neal-Marshall Black Culture Cent
 er<br />  275 N. Eagleson Avenue\, Bloomington\, Indiana</p><p>  Drawing 
 on recent experiments with AI for the Humanities Indicators and a study o
 f historians' adoption of earlier technologies\, Townsend will discuss AI
 's strengths and limitations in humanities research\, how it might redraw
  the boundary between assistance and expertise in the field\, and why the
 se experiments have nevertheless made him more optimistic about the futur
 e of the humanities.</p><p><strong>Robert Townsend</strong> oversees Huma
 nities\, Arts\, and Culture programs and day-to-day work on the <a href="
 https://www.amacad.org/humanities-indicators">Humanities Indicators</a> a
 t the <a href="https://www.amacad.org/">American Academy of Arts and Scie
 nces</a>. Prior to the Academy\, he spent 24 years at the American Histor
 ical Association\, in positions ranging from editorial assistant to deput
 y director. He is the author of <em><a href="https://press.uchicago.edu/u
 cp/books/book/chicago/H/bo14312740.html">History's Babel: Scholarship\, P
 rofessionalization\, and the Historical Enterprise in the United States\,
  1880-1940</a></em> (University of Chicago Press\, 2013) and author or co
 -author of over 200 articles on various aspects of history\, higher educa
 tion\, and public humanities.</p><p><strong>Save these dates for more in 
 the series:</strong></p><p><strong>February 5: Claire Katz</strong><br />
   President\, Board of Directors\, National Humanities Alliance</p><p><st
 rong>March 24: Phillip Brian Harper</strong><br />  Vice President\, Prog
 rams and Higher Learning\, Mellon Foundation</p><p><strong>April 8: Joy C
 onnolly</strong><br />  President\, American Council of Learned Societies
 </p><p>  These talks are presented by the College Arts + Humanities Insti
 tute with generous assistance from the College of Arts + Sciences and the
  Ruth N. Halls Humanities Fund. For more information\, please visit <a hr
 ef="https://cahi.indiana.edu">cahi.indiana.edu</a>. If need assistance or
  if you have an accommodation request\, please email <a href="mailto:mail
 to:cahi@iu.edu">cahi@iu.edu</a> or call 812-856-1169.</p><p>  For those w
 ho cannot attend in person\, a Zoom option will be available. Please emai
 l <a href="mailto:cahi@iu.edu">cahi@iu.edu</a> for the Zoom link.</p>
